A||||2||grinthock||Car is smoother -- Problems resolved!||||1||
Z||000000||grinthock||07-28-2001||08:08 AM||||Well after freaking out with this sputtering problem and stalling issue it's now fixed...<P>The IAC had carboned up, when you start the car the IAC moves a bit and I noticed it was struggling, so I got out the old carb clean and sprayed both INSIDE the plenum and the IAC hole and pintle.  LOTS of black stuff came out of both, I jacked the car up on the right side to help all the carb clean get out of the system, WOW!! VERY black, sprayed for almost 3 minutes until the black stopped pouring out,  car now runs VERY clean, smoother acceleration, and WAY more torque, I guess it was pretty bad in there.....<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jay||07-28-2001||10:50 AM||||Yes, something that is many times overlooked!<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cayuga3_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||07-28-2001||12:12 PM||||Carbon in there will do nasty things....<BR>As bad as you said it was... <P>Keep an eye on it over the next day or so. Then keep this in mind later if the IAC seems to be messing up again.<P>You said Carb cleaner.... That stuff cleans great but many of them aren't to kind to electrical stuff. Next time use spray injector cleaner. It's more friendly to plastic EFI parts.<P>If the problems return take the IAC out and check it. It may be stuff inside the IAC motor. Some cleaners can damage the guts of the IAC if they got inside it. Others can soften any gooey dirt but if they didn't get it all out then the thing can goo up again later.||||reg||1
